  "content": {
    "title": "ACC tournament championship score, highlights: Duke hangs on for conference title",
    "text": "Updated March 14, 2026, 11:25 p.m. ETIt was never easy, but Duke got its second straight ACC Tournament title against the Virginia Cavaliers on March 14.After a tepid first half from possible future No. 1 overall pick Cameron Boozer, the Blue Devils got 11 second-half points from their star player in a 74-70 win. During Boozer's slow first half, his brother Cayden was able to pick up the slack, scoring 14 first-half points before a quiet second half. Isaiah Evans dropped 20, including a go-ahead 3-pointer late in the game and the free throws that would eventually ice it for the Blue Devils.Even with injuries to Caleb Evans and Patrick Ngongba, Duke was able to hold on in a physical game with a shortened seven-man rotation. Nikolas Khamenia played 32 minutes for Duke off the bench and scored nine, leaving his own stamp on the victory.For Virginia, it's the end of what has been a remarkable conference run under first-year coach Ryan Odom. Ugonna Onyenso had another ridiculous defensive game, logging nine blocks, an ACC Championship record. It caps off a tournament in which he set the record for blocks in the bracket as well. Thijs De Ridder was uncharacteristically quiet, scoring just five points, while Malik Thomas and Sam Lewis led the way with 18 and 17 points, respectively.Duke will now turn its attention to the NCAA Tournament, where it will be a No. 1 seed, while Virginia will do the same.USA TODAY Sports followed Duke and Virginia live. Look below for highlights and analysis of Duke's conference title-clinching game.ACC championship live scoreTEAMH1H2FVirginia363470Duke383674ACC championship live updates Duke survives to win ACC TournamentDuke holds on to beat Virginia and win the ACC Tournament. A great effort down the stretch from Cameron Boozer and Isaiah Evans in particular gives the Blue Devils their second straight ACC Tournament championship, capping off a really impressive season for Scheyer's Blue Devils. Their focus now turns to the NCAA Tournament, where they'll look for their first title since 2015. Duke up two possessions with under two minutesIt's not much, but Duke has the tiniest bit of breathing room. The Blue Devils lead 70-66 with 1:51 left, and Virginia takes a timeout as the energy in the arena hits a fever pitch. Duke retakes the lead, Virginia responds and ties itA 3-pointer from Isaiah Evans out of a duke timeout gives the Blue Devils a 66-63 lead. Virginia, however, scores and splits free throws to tie it up for the 12th time. Duke then scores yet again to make it 68-66, 2:30 left. Cameron Boozer finally makes an impactIt's been a quiet night for Cameron Boozer, but he finally starts to break through for Duke down the stretch. First he rips an offensive rebound from Onyenso and puts it away for an and-1. Then he gets another two points at the line to put Duke up 63-60. 5:43 remaining. Virginia and Duke tied with under 8 minutes remainingVirginia ties the game with a triple from Sam Lewis. Cameron Boozer is struggling, shooting only 2-of-11 from the field with six points. He's trying to shoot through it, but it hasn't been his night. Virginia, meanwhile, has been at its best pushing the pace. Both teams looking for separationThese two teams are playing TOUGH. Duke reclaims the lead near the 12-minute mark in the half, but nothing has come easy. Virginia's Ugonna Onyenso has seven blocks on the night, and he is dominating the paint. Duke leads 53-51 with 11:51 remaining. Second half opens strong for VirginiaAfter a rough shooting showing at times in the first half, Virginia looks to have found some stroke. The Cavaliers hit three 3s and continue their run, jumping out to a 49-45 lead going into the half's first TV timeout. Duke goes to the bench looking for answers. ACC championship first half statsDuke shoots 50% from the floor in the first half, and despite a sluggish start from beyond the arc, the Blue Devils 26 points in the paint are proving to be an early difference-maker. They're out-rebounding the Cavaliers 19-12 and have nine offensive boards. Virginia, for its part, is shooting 46%. Cayden Boozer leads both teams with 14 points and is two points off his career-high set in the semifinals March 13, while Dallin Hall and Malik Thomas lead Virginia with seven points apiece. Virginia ends first half on 7-2 runDick Vitale said how big the last two minutes of the half would be when Virginia went down 36-29, and the Hoos answer the call. They end the half on a 7-2 run to go into the locker room down 38-36, with Dallin Hall scoring five and shooting a look at the Duke bench. Cayden Boozer making a difference earlyWhile the accolades so often go to his brother Cameron, Cayden Boozer has been a difference-maker early in this game. He leads all scorers with 12 and is 4-for-7 from the field. Duke leads 29-27 with four minutes in the half. Virginia, Duke going blow for blowWe get some lead changes in rapid succession, as both sides start to find their footing offensively. Duke takes a 20-18 lead before a Virginia triple gives the Hoos the lead. Duke answers with another bucket, before Virginia scores again to reclaim a 23-22 lead. Duke then follows up with a bucket in transition to go up 24-23. Both teams are battling in the latter part of the first half. Defense winning the day earlyAs Duke goes to the free throw line with a chance to take the lead in a 14-13 game, both teams are struggling from the field early. Slightly over eight minutes in, Virginia is 43% from the field to Duke's 36%, and the teams are a combined 3-of-14 from three. Virginia jumps out to early leadDespite a few quick buckets for Duke, Virginia enters the first TV timeout with a 9-6 lead thanks to four points from Sam Lewis and a triple from Malik Thomas. Cayden Boozer and Dame Starr have early buckets for the Blue Devils. Cameron Boozer statsCam Boozer has been among the best players in the country, with the superstar and prospective No. 1 overall pick in next year's NBA Draft averaging 22.8 points per game and 10.3 rebounds. He will look to continue that success against a talented UVA team. When did Virginia last win ACC Tournament?Virginia's last ACC Tournament win was 2018, with Tony Bennett still prowling the Cavalier sidelines. Ryan Odom is looking to win the conference in his first season against Jon Scheyer and the vaunted Blue Devils.What channel is the ACC tournament championship on today?TV channel: ESPNStreaming: ESPN app, Fubo (free trial)Duke vs. Virginia men's basketball will be broadcast on ESPN, with streaming options available on the ESPN app, which requires a valid cable login to access, and Fubo, which offers a free trial to potential subscribers.ACC tournament championship start timeDate: Saturday, March 14Start time: 8:30 p.m. ETLocation: Spectrum Center (Charlotte)The Blue Devils and Cavaliers are set to tip off at 8:30 p.m. ET on Saturday, March 14 in the ACC tournament championship game at the Spectrum Center in Charlotte, the home of the NBA's Charlotte Hornets.ACC tournament championship picks, predictions, oddsOdds from BetMGMSpread: Duke (-6.5)Over/under: 139.5 pointsMoneyline: Duke (-295), Virginia (+230)Prediction: Duke 78, Virginia 69The Hoos will manage to keep it closer than they did two weeks ago in Durham, but even without Foster and Ngongba, the Blue Devils have more than enough talent to get past Virginia and further stake their claim to the No. 1 overall seed in the NCAA tournament.",
